REGIONAL REGULATION OF NORTH CENTRAL TIMOR REGENCY

NUMBER 3 OF 2020

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF NORTH CENTRAL TIMOR,

Considering:

a. that every person has equal dignity and worth to live in society, nation and state and has the right to obtain protection of human rights and fundamental human freedoms without discrimination as an effort to realize gender equality and justice in national and regional development;
b. that in order to realize gender equality and justice in development in North Central Timor Regency, there is a need to increase the effectiveness and optimization of the implementation of the women's empowerment sub-affairs as an inseparable part of the functional activities of government and non-government institutions in order to improve the position, role and quality of women in development in North Central Timor Regency;
c. that to guarantee legal certainty and to fill the legal vacuum in order to realize gender equality and justice in development in North Central Timor Regency, there is a need for regulation in a Regional Regulation concerning the implementation of women's empowerment;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Women's Empowerment;

In View of:

1 Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 69 of 1958 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions within the Level I Regions of Bali, West Nusa Tenggara and East Nusa Tenggara (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1958 Number 122 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1655);
3. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587); As amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

With Mutual Approval of

THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF NORTH CENTRAL TIMOR REGENCY

and

REGENT OF NORTH CENTRAL TIMOR

HAS DECIDED:

To Enact:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT.

Article 13

1. The Regional Government is responsible for preparing planning of policies, programs and development activities with a Gender perspective.
2. The responsibility as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be coordinated by the PD that administers governmental affairs in the field of development planning, research and Regional development.
3. The planning as referred to in paragraph (1) includes stages:
a. Musrenbangdus;
b. Musrembangdes/kel;
c. Musrembangcam;
d. Musrenbang at regional level;
e. preparation of RPJMD;
f. preparation of PD Renstra;
g. preparation of PD Renja; and
h. RKPD;
i. preparation of RAPBD;
4. The planning as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through Gender analysis using the Gender Analysis Pathway method or other analysis methods.
5. The implementation of Gender analysis on the RPJMD and PD Renstra may cooperate with Higher Education institutions or other parties who have capability in their field.

Article 7

The duties of the PUG Working Group include:
a. promoting and facilitating dialogue among work units within the scope of regional government;
b. developing work networks in accordance with the main duties, functions and authorities given by the leadership in efforts to realize gender equality and justice;
c. assigning the technical team to carry out the preparation of the PUG RAD in order to realize gender equality and justice adjusted to the periodization of the RPJMD;
d. elaborating and following up on policies and implementation programs to be gender responsive that are implicit and explicit in the RPJMD;
e. coordinating the establishment of PUG Focal Points in every PD or work unit;
f. promoting PUG in every PD;
g. conducting capacity strengthening for PUG Focal Points in preparing gender-responsive budgets by the technical team;
h. overseeing the preparation of gender-responsive planning and budgets in their respective PDs;
i. including gender indicators in the guidelines for preparing the APBD;
j. ensuring that gender-responsive budgets become a consideration in the discussion and determination of the APBD together with the DPRD;
k. holding coordination meetings every 6 (six) months to ensure the implementation of PUG in the Region;
l. making and submitting reports on the implementation of PUG to the regent every 6 (six) months; and
m. holding coordination meetings before the preparation of the RKPD.

Article 39

1. Facilitation of the establishment of service provider institutions as referred to in Article 36 paragraph (2) letter a shall be implemented by establishing family learning centers.
2. The stages of establishing family learning centers include:
a. preparing regulations;
b. preparing human resources;
c. preparing facilities and infrastructure; and
d. establishment of family learning centers.
3. Family learning centers function:
a. as service institutions in prevention and counseling;
b. as a referral liaison point as a solution for child and family problems;
c. strengthening family capacity in caring for and protecting children;
d. as a place for family learning through education for parents; and
e. as a holistic integrative family one-stop service based on children's rights.

Article 28

1. PD that administers governmental affairs in the field of cooperatives, small and medium enterprises and industrial trade is responsible for facilitating Women's Empowerment in the economic field.
2. Implementation of Women's Empowerment in the economic field is carried out through:
a. provision of skills and job training;
b. facilitation of the formation of productive economic business groups;
c. facilitation of strengthening and development of productive economic business groups;
d. facilitation and capital assistance; and
e. facilitation of marketing network development.

